WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.154
TypeScript checks source during
development and erases types when code is compiled.

2
00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:10.017
A statement that casts req.body as
UpdateProfileRequest performs no runtime parsing,

3
00:00:10.097 --> 00:00:13.135
removes no extra keys, and proves nothing
about values.

4
00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:17.546
Treat an HTTP body as unknown until a
runtime schema or equivalent validator has

5
00:00:17.626 --> 00:00:19.759
produced a trusted application input.

6
00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:22.489
The schema should express the actual
operation:

7
00:00:22.569 --> 00:00:26.382
required and optional properties, strings
and length limits, enums,

8
00:00:26.462 --> 00:00:29.630
nullable values, array bounds, and nested
object shapes.

9
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.972
Libraries such as Zod, Valibot, Joi, Ajv,

10
00:00:32.052 --> 00:00:35.349
or framework-native validators can support
this boundary.

11
00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:37.432
The important behavior is explicit:

12
00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:41.067
know whether unknown properties cause
rejection, are stripped,

13
00:00:41.147 --> 00:00:42.440
or remain in the result,

14
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.528
and use the validated result rather than
returning to raw req.body later.

15
00:00:46.608 --> 00:00:51.197
Strict rejection is often useful for
update APIs because it exposes client mistakes

16
00:00:51.277 --> 00:00:53.992
and attempts to send fields outside the
contract.

17
00:00:54.072 --> 00:00:57.045
Deliberate stripping can be valid for
compatibility,

18
00:00:57.125 --> 00:01:00.809
but it must not create uncertainty about
which object is persisted.

19
00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:03.152
Neither mode replaces explicit mapping.

20
00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:06.076
If a schema is generated from a broad
database model,

21
00:01:06.156 --> 00:01:08.870
or if validated data is later mixed with
raw input,

22
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.600
the mass-assignment boundary remains too
wide.

23
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.654
Treat coercion and defaults as contract
behavior too;

24
00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:18.871
an automatic conversion can create a state
change the caller did not express

25
00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:19.468
clearly.

26
00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:23.620
Validation, field permission, and business
authorization are separate.

27
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.190
Validation establishes that displayName is
a permitted shape.

28
00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.795
The operation contract establishes that
profile editing may modify displayName.

29
00:01:31.875 --> 00:01:35.559
Authorization establishes which profile
this principal may edit.

30
00:01:35.639 --> 00:01:38.677
Business rules may add state-dependent
restrictions.

31
00:01:38.757 --> 00:01:41.601
A database constraint can enforce some
invariants,

32
00:01:41.681 --> 00:01:45.753
but it does not know the endpoint's
intended field set or caller authority.

33
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:48.096
Use runtime validation as the entry gate,

34
00:01:48.176 --> 00:01:51.860
then carry only its narrow output into
explicit application logic.
